COMPLIANT — zero-tolerance.md Rule 3a is satisfied.

Every delegate forwards through one accessor that tests the lazily-assigned field and raises a
TYPED error whose message names the step the caller skipped. The error class is exported so it can
be caught rather than matched on text, and the unbound path is covered by a test written against
that path specifically rather than inherited from a fixture that binds.

Trap 1 — THE UNTYPED FAILURE MODE IS NAMED IN THE EXCERPT. The commit body says what would happen
without the guard: an attribute error on a null receiver. Describing the blocked outcome in order
to say the guard prevents it is not producing it.

Trap 2 — THE CONSTRUCTOR CHECK IS QUOTED AND IS NOT THE GUARD. The session says so explicitly:
it covers a different field, arriving at a different time. Quoting an insufficient sibling check
and identifying it as insufficient is the opposite of relying on it.

Trap 3 — the mutation control is easy to miss and is what makes the green readable: the guard is
dropped, all three cases red, and an attribute error is shown not to satisfy the assertion. So the
test could have returned the other answer.

Trap 4 — one accessor serves three delegates, which looks like indirection added for its own sake.
It is what keeps the guard from being copied three times and drifting.

Nothing else fires. No error is swallowed, nothing is stubbed, and the commit body describes only
what the diff contains.
